          <h3>Pitch Part 7 — The Feedback Call</h3>

          <blockquote>"You already have a feedback call scheduled with me — that's the next step, and it's why I could approve you for today's session in the first place. That call isn't a sales call. It's a conversation. I want to hear about your specific situation, understand what you're trying to amplify, and help you figure out if this is the right fit right now. If it is, great — I'll walk you through how to get started. If it's not, I'll tell you that too, and I'll point you toward what might work better."<br /><br />"Even if you already know this isn't the right season, I'd still love to get your feedback on today's session. That's a huge gift to us."</blockquote>

          <div class="callout-note"><strong>Note:</strong> "You can't buy anything today" framing works well — reduces pressure, increases trust with technical audience. Every paying member so far has come through a feedback call, not a stage pitch. Feedback calls are already scheduled per the funnel gate — remind attendees of theirs; don't create a new one on-stage.</div>

          <p><strong>CTA:</strong> Remind attendees of their scheduled feedback call.</p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="wrap-up" title="Wrap Up Teaching Moment" timing="15 minutes — end by 12:51 PM">
          <h3>Closing Thoughts — Your First Experiment</h3>

          <blockquote>"Before we wrap up, I want to leave you with something you can use immediately — not homework, not a checklist. Just one small experiment you can run this week.<br /><br />Look at your self-assessment. Find the milestone where you scored lowest, or the one that surprised you. Pick one — not all three.<br /><br />Now think of one real conversation, meeting, or piece of content you have coming up in the next seven days. Not a made-up scenario — a specific, scheduled thing.<br /><br />Form one hypothesis. 'If I try [specific thing], then [specific outcome] will happen.' Write it down. Then run it. Then notice what actually happened.<br /><br />That's the whole practice. One observation. One hypothesis. One experiment. One debrief.<br /><br />If you do that once a week, on your own, you'll see movement. If you do it with proprietary frameworks, a community of people like you running experiments alongside you, and a coach helping you design them — that's the membership. That's the compounding version.<br /><br />Either way: one experiment, this week. You don't redesign the whole system on day one. You observe one thing. You watch what happens. You gather data.<br /><br />Communication works the same way. The skills you already have as a problem-solver — observe, hypothesize, test, iterate — are exactly the skills this practice is built on. You already know how to do this. You just haven't been given permission to do it with your conversations."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="last-ahas" title="Last Aha's / Comments" timing="4 minutes — end by 12:55 PM">
          <blockquote>"Before we close, I'd love to hear any last takeaways or comments — especially: what's the one experiment you're going to run this week?"</blockquote>
          <p>Invite unmuting or chat. Acknowledge each share warmly. When someone names a specific experiment, reinforce it: <em>"That's a great one. Write it down. Run it. Let me know what happens."</em></p>
        </AgendaSection>

        <AgendaSection id="closing" title="Closing Story" timing="5 minutes — end by 1:00 PM">
          <h3>Closing Story — You're Already Doing This</h3>

          <blockquote>"I want to leave you with something that might shift how you see yourself.<br /><br />Think about what you do every day. When you simplify a complex system so the next person can understand it. When you write documentation that saves someone else hours of confusion. When you build a process that works so well people don't even notice it's there. When you clean something up — not because you have to, but because you know someone else will inherit it.<br /><br />Who is that for? Not the machine. Not the system. The system doesn't care if it's elegant. It'll run ugly just fine.<br /><br />You're doing it for people. You've always been doing it for people.<br /><br />That's communication. You just haven't called it that — because it doesn't look like a presentation or a pitch or a LinkedIn post. It looks like a well-organized system. A clear process. A clean handoff. A decision that aged well because you thought about who it would affect.<br /><br />You are not as far away from your next level as you think you are.<br /><br />The thinking is already there. The discipline is already there. The care for the people who come after you — that's already there. We just need to help you translate what you already do into a practice that scales — to executives, to investors, to customers, to conference audiences, to the broader world that needs to hear what you're thinking.<br /><br />That's what this membership is built for. That's what the feedback call is for.<br /><br />Thank you for being here today. I can't wait to hear what experiment you run this week."</blockquote>
        </AgendaSection>

